WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.320
- I'm Paul North.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.780
I teach in the German Department at Yale.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.510
This is a series

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:13.920
at the Whitney Humanities Center

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.890
called "The Value of Marx's Capital."

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.750
These are the Franke
Lectures in the Humanities

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.530
at the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.670
I need to do some thank yous.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.920
First of all, the Franke
Lectures are made possible

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.640
by the generosity of
Richard and Barbara Franke

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.210
and are intended to
present important topics

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.220
in the humanities to a
wide and general audience.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.660
I'd like to thank Alice Kaplan,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.460
the director of the
Whitney Humanities Center

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.430
for making possible what seemed

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.100
to have been potentially
impossible this year.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.610
So thank you, Alice.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.850
To Sandra Malan Bowles and
Leana Hirschfeld-Kroen

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.930
for doing the organization,

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.890
and to Audrey Leak our intrepid,

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.603
brilliant undergraduate
tech assistant, thank you.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.660
The workshop is gonna run like this.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.360
We'll have a presentation
from our speaker,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.390
and then we'll do questions.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.620
I'm gonna ask you to hold
your questions if you can

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.320
until after the speaker speaks

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.270
and then to put them in the chat.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.300
We'll then go through the
chat and open up your mics

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.653
when it's time for you
to read your question.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.453
If you put in questions earlier,
they're likely to get lost.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.630
I like to start with a quote
from a text from the speaker.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:29.720
So I want to start from with
a quote from our speaker

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.810
from the "Boston Review"
in February of 2018,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:36.407
quote, "Asking what the
slave trade says about Marx

00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:38.807
"requires that we start where Marx did,

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:41.727
"with analysis of the commodity form.

00:01:41.727 --> 00:01:44.840
"But we need to replace
the bolt of linen Marx used

00:01:45.687 --> 00:01:48.517
"as the iconic object of that analysis.

00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:50.317
"Not with slave-cultivated cotton

00:01:50.317 --> 00:01:51.150
"that was more central

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.757
"to 19th century industrial manufacturing

00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:55.737
"or even the slave-grown sugar

00:01:55.737 --> 00:01:57.187
"that fueled the colonial wealth,

00:01:57.187 --> 00:01:58.957
"Marx erroneously identified

00:01:58.957 --> 00:02:02.307
"as part of capitalism's prehistory.

00:02:02.307 --> 00:02:05.270
"To heed Johnson's call to quote,

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.317
"'Use the history of slaves as the source

00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:09.097
"'rather than the subject
of knowledge,' unquote

00:02:09.097 --> 00:02:11.577
"we will have to instead put
the enslaved human herself

00:02:11.577 --> 00:02:14.570
"at the center of our analysis
of the commodity form."

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.290
When I read the sentence,
I was really galvanized

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:20.120
and invited Stephanie
Smallwood to join us.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.290
We're really grateful that you're here.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.690
Stephanie Smallwood is
associate professor of history

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.790
and comparative history of ideas

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.090
at the University of Washington, Seattle,

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.860
where she's also a faculty associate

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:33.160
of the Harry Bridges
Center for Labor Studies.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.820
Her research and teaching
explore early modern histories

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.060
of slavery, colonialism, and race

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.000
with particular emphasis on
the transatlantic slave trade,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.751
racial capitalism and African
diasporas in the Americas.

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:46.960
As far as I can see,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.320
she's the most interesting
political economist

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.610
of slavery going.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.747
She's the author of "Saltwater Slavery:

00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:58.590
"A Middle Passage from
Africa to American Diaspora"

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.810
from 2007, which won the
2008 Frederick Douglass prize

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.073
from Yale's own Gilder Lehrman Center.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.420
She's at work on a project

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.867
called "Africa in the Atlantic World:

00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:10.657
"A Geopolitical History."

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.193
And we're really happy
to have you and welcome.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.282
- Thank you so much.

00:03:18.282 --> 00:03:21.190
It's an honor to be here,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.940
an honor to spend the evening

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.743
discussing the value of capital.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.740
And I'll just say that I am relieved,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.470
not quite fully relieved,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.850
but happy to be starting this conversation

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:40.580
knowing that that thing that
we're all watching obsessively

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:44.610
has started to inch in
the right direction.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:49.459
So I can kind of leave my
obsession with the election

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:52.293
and focus on what we're here for today.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.240
Thank you, Paul, for that quote.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.610
It is a perfect segue, I think,

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.263
into what I want to do today.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.790
I wanna start by saying a little bit

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:07.193
about how I've come to
develop a deep interest

00:04:07.193 --> 00:04:10.100
in the Marxian account of capital.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.170
It's not an obvious set
of interests for me,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.490
since I'm actually not a specialist

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:20.130
in political theory or political economy,

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.370
but as a historian of slavery

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.460
and of the transatlantic slave trade

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.500
and one whose research
has focused particularly

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.500
on how the early modern
Atlantic system organized itself

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.960
around the production and circulation

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.350
of people as commodities,

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.190
I guess it became inevitable

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:46.260
that eventually I would
have to engage with Marx.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.870
So off and on over the years

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.100
since I published "Saltwater Slavery,"

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.250
I've dabbled in the
debates on the relationship

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.233
of capitalism to slavery.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.660
And I've done so always a bit confused

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.660
and bemused by the ways
those debates rarely extend

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:11.110
beyond questions about 19th
century antebellum slavery

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:12.533
in the U.S.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.730
The question that follows
me around relentlessly

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.750
whenever I'm deep into Marx's oeuvre

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.130
concerns the relationship of capitalism

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.073
to the transatlantic world of slavery

00:05:27.073 --> 00:05:28.703
in the 16th, 17th, and 18th centuries.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.660
So my questions have less
to do with the entanglements

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.660
of British abolitionism, cotton
slavery in the U.S. South

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.483
and the rise of industrial
capitalism in the 19th century

00:05:42.483 --> 00:05:47.483
and more to do with how to
make sense of the long history

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.790
of what Marx called the capitalist era,

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:53.763
beginning in the 16th century.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:58.370
So with the thoughts I'm
going share this afternoon

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.850
or this evening for you all,

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:04.850
I want to problematize a central assertion

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.430
of Marx's account of capital,

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.170
and that's namely his insistence

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:14.300
that the circumstances in which
money could be transformed

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.510
into capital were limited
to the time and place

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:23.370
of 19th century industrialization
in Northern Europe.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:24.530
And I want to suggest

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:28.000
that the histories of
transatlantic circulation

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.740
and enslavement of African people must be

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:35.720
at the center of any adequate
account of capitalism.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.440
And in doing so, I'm gonna propose

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.780
that we need to rethink
the Marxian account

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:43.190
of New World slavery

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:46.963
as capital, so-called
primitive accumulation.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.110
A useful place to begin,

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:55.530
I think, is to remind
ourselves why Marx insisted

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:59.480
on framing the conditions of possibility

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:03.620
for the capital relation to
emerge as narrowly as he did.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.470
And on that point, I want us to remember

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.110
that the revolution he was trying

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.200
to eliminate was not the
production of surplus value itself.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.533
That's not what was
special about capitalism.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:22.350
More precisely, it was the
money owner's discovery

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:26.630
that he can generate
surplus value in perpetuity

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:30.370
by the appropriation of a
particular form of labor,

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.460
which of course, as we know,
from our reading of "Capital"

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.833
was the specific form of free labor.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.090
So I'm gonna go ahead and share my screen

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:44.793
and pull up the first slide.

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:55.610
Marx elaborated the conditions

00:07:55.610 --> 00:08:00.040
that made labor free, very precisely.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.350
What the capital relation required,

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:05.830
according to him, was
labor power's presence

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.350
in the market as a commodity.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:10.320
And we can use this passage

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.600
from his discussion of the sale
and purchase of labor power,

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:18.100
one of several very
explicit statements on this,

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.810
to recall Marx's precise words

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.630
regarding the four conditions

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.220
that labor's appearance

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:28.113
in the market as a commodity required.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:33.260
Number one, it had to
be sold as a commodity

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.170
by its possessor.

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:39.520
And so we get Marx telling us
that labor power can appear

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.970
on the market as a commodity only if,

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:45.770
and in so far as its possessor,

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:49.070
the individual whose labor power it is,

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.543
offers it for sale or
sells it as a commodity.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:56.320
Second, he tells us that the possessor

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:59.660
of labor power has to have full ownership

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.010
of his labor power.

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:03.230
He must possess himself.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:04.297
And so Marx writes,

00:09:04.297 --> 00:09:07.487
"The possessor must
have it at his disposal.

00:09:07.487 --> 00:09:09.787
"He must be the free proprietor

00:09:09.787 --> 00:09:14.787
"of his own labor capacity,
hence of his person."

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:18.930
Third, the two parties
to this transaction,

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:22.190
the owner of money and
the owner of labor power,

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.900
must be equals, juridically at least.

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:28.600
And so Marx insists

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:33.403
that both have to be equal
in the eyes of the law.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:38.850
And lastly, the system's
infinite momentum, right?

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:42.090
That essential feature
that is capital's ability

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:47.090
to reproduce valorization
in perpetuity requires

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:51.430
that the possessor of
labor power be compelled

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.970
to return to the market
over and over again.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.730
And so it is necessary that
he sell his labor power

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.830
only for a delimited period of time.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.957
And on this, Marx writes,

00:10:02.957 --> 00:10:05.557
"For this relation to continue,

00:10:05.557 --> 00:10:10.047
"the proprietor of labor
power must always sell it

00:10:10.047 --> 00:10:13.710
"for a limited period only."

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:14.940
Why?

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:19.290
Because if he were to sell the
totality of his labor power

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:20.750
all at once,

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:24.900
he would effectively make
himself into an enslaved person.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:28.527
And so Marx concludes this passage saying,

00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:32.837
"For if he were to sell it
in a lump, once and for all,

00:10:32.837 --> 00:10:35.127
"he would be selling himself,

00:10:35.127 --> 00:10:39.237
"converting himself from
a free man into a slave,

00:10:39.237 --> 00:10:44.237
"from an owner of a
commodity into a commodity."

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:50.010
And so with this, we have
Marx's central axiom,

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:55.010
that capital's endless
self-valorization requires

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:57.230
not just the labor power,

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.550
that labor power itself
become a commodity,

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:05.450
but more precisely, that it
appear repeatedly in the market.

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:08.550
And further, that this can only occur

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:13.460
when labor power is brought to
the market by the free person

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:15.380
in whom it is embedded,

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:19.373
what he referred to as alienation by sale.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.520
So the African enslaved worker does not

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.840
and indeed cannot appear
as a central protagonist

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.720
in Marx's account of capital.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.280
For Marx, if the laborer
in question is enslaved,

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.000
a fully-commoditized person,

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.113
we can no longer be in the
terrain of the capital relation.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.270
If the circulation of labor power,

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.470
freely alienated by sale

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.690
is the necessary condition of possibility

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.700
for the emergence of the capital relation,

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:58.700
what I want to ask is the
place of Atlantic slavery

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:03.290
and its circulation of labor
power alienated by coercion

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:04.653
and capital's story?

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.780
As someone who studies the
commoditization of people,

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.180
the full commoditization of people,

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:16.000
and the circulation of commoditized
people for their labor,

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.330
this is what I want to
keep open as a question.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:22.690
I want to ask whether
slavery as capitalism

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:26.250
is as unthinkable as Marx insisted.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:29.630
And what I'll try to do
today is persuade you

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:33.084
that enslaved Africans can
and should be at the center

00:12:33.084 --> 00:12:36.243
of our account of modern capital.

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.910
So I'm gonna organize my
thoughts for this evening

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.010
around two questions.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:48.010
Number one, what did Marx
think he knew about slavery,

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:51.330
and number two, what did Marx
need to know about slavery

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:52.833
but failed to ask?

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:55.370
So the first question,

00:12:55.370 --> 00:13:00.300
what did Marx think he knew about slavery?

