00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:53.540
- Okay, now I'm seeing it.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.063
Yep, I got it.
- Okay.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:58.070
- And that's the one that I deleted.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:15:59.253
So the next slide.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:02.840
Yes, there we go.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:05.783
Perfect, thank you so much.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.820
Okay.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:13.650
So what did Marx think
he knew about slavery?

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:16.420
Well before he had begun the research

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:19.450
at the British Museum that
would become "Capital,"

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.160
Marx had already identified slavery

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:24.100
as an important part

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.940
of the story of 19th
century industrialization.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:31.240
In his 1847 "Poverty of Philosophy,"

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:35.320
he famously wrote that direct slavery

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:38.530
is just as much the pivot
of bourgeois industry

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:41.560
as machinery, credits, et cetera.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:44.010
Without slavery, you have no cotton.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:47.210
Without cotton, you
have no modern industry.

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:50.770
It is slavery that has given
the colonies their value.

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:54.290
It is the colonies that
have created world trade,

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:55.680
and it is world trade

00:16:55.680 --> 00:17:00.347
that is the precondition
of large-scale industry.

00:17:00.347 --> 00:17:04.787
"Thus," he writes, "slavery
is an economic category

00:17:04.787 --> 00:17:07.137
"of the greatest importance."

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:13.520
Marx knew the value slavery
gave to the New World colonies,

00:17:13.690 --> 00:17:18.000
because he was reading the
current historical literature

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.920
on English colonizing,
works by Herman Merivale,

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.000
for example, that explained

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.360
which colonies were producing commodities

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:27.017
for the international market

00:17:27.017 --> 00:17:28.490
and which ones weren't

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:31.850
and told him of Jamaica's
status, for instance,

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:35.460
as the greatest sugar-growing
country in the world.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:38.560
That is to say Marx knew
the aggregate measure

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:41.140
of the wealth returned to Europe

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:45.840
by the importation and consumption
of colonial commodities.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.860
And it was in this
idiom that he understood

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:52.033
the value slavery gave to the colonies.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:57.010
And it's in this sense
also that Marx assigned

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:01.180
to New World slavery the role
of contributing substantially

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:05.100
to capital's era of
primitive accumulation,

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:07.850
the amassing of capital and labor power

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.340
that had taken place

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:13.650
outside the cyclical movement of capital.

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:16.830
The historical break,
we might say, or shift

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:20.457
that as Marx put it was
quote, "Not the result

00:18:20.457 --> 00:18:22.647
"of the capitalist mode of production,

00:18:22.647 --> 00:18:25.307
"but rather its point of departure."

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:29.290
What Marx characterized

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:33.170
as the treasures captured outside Europe

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:37.160
by undisguised looting,
enslavement and murder

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.730
that flowed back to the mother country

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:42.883
and were turned into capital there.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:45.103
Next slide, please.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:50.350
And so in this sharply worded

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:52.890
and often quoted statement,

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:56.060
we get Marx's most explicit observation

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:58.520
regarding the multiple

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:01.570
and global appropriations
he's characterized

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:05.750
as capital's prehistory
of primitive accumulation.

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:09.860
He writes that the discovery
of gold and silver in America,

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:14.670
the extirpation, enslavement
and entombment in mines

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:17.850
of the indigenous population
of that continent,

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:21.800
the beginnings of the
conquest and plunder of India

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:25.370
and the conversion of
Africa into a preserve

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:28.820
for the commercial hunting of black skins

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:32.980
are all things which characterize the dawn

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:36.327
of the era of capitalist production.

00:19:36.327 --> 00:19:40.037
"These idyllic proceedings,"
he says ironically,

00:19:40.037 --> 00:19:43.377
"are the chief moments of
primitive accumulation."

00:19:44.728 --> 00:19:49.000
But if it wasn't as an economic category

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.540
that the particular historical instance

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:55.400
of New World slavery was an important part

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:59.090
of capital's story, what, I want to ask,

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:03.240
about slavery as a category of labor?

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.188
we should notice in this passage

00:20:06.188 --> 00:20:10.940
that Marx has invoked European
slave trading in Africa,

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:13.710
the commercial hunting of black skins,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:18.360
but he has not explicitly
named the New World plantation

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.530
and its expropriation of African labor.

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:25.170
Instead here, it is the mineral wealth

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:27.370
and the expropriation of indigenous labor

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.420
that occupies his interest in slavery

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:33.973
as a mode of production
in the colonized Americas.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:39.470
For a 19th Century European
political economy general,

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:43.010
generally speaking, slavery was a static

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:45.510
and unchanging mode of production.

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.670
And as such, it was understood

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:51.220
to have certain universal characteristics.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.520
As a system of labor exploitation,

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:58.020
slavery was understood to claim
the spoils of surplus labor,

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:00.950
the same as any system
that concentrated ownership

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:02.680
of the means of production

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:05.720
in the hands of a dominant social class.

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:08.730
But, and this is significant,

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:11.650
conventional wisdom had it that slavery

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:16.650
would not turn surplus labor
into surplus value or profit.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:20.910
Because slave labor power was part

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:22.630
of the means of production,

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.780
it would not be subjected

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:28.270
to the scale of exploitation
and abuse associated

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:32.310
with the drive to profit
from labor surplus.

00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:36.660
The expectation then was that
owners would not be inclined

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:38.980
to direct slave labor power

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:41.960
toward production from market exchange.

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:45.380
And so slavery was
expected to limit itself

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.720
to the production of use values

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:52.270
but not approach the
production of exchange values.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:53.573
Next slide, please.

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.820
Marx explained it this way.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:01.647
He wrote, "It is clear that
in any economic formation

00:22:01.647 --> 00:22:04.177
"of society, where they use value

00:22:04.177 --> 00:22:06.687
"rather than the exchange value

00:22:06.687 --> 00:22:08.717
"of the product predominates,

00:22:08.717 --> 00:22:11.547
"surplus labor will be restricted

00:22:11.547 --> 00:22:14.717
"by a more or less confined set of needs.

00:22:14.717 --> 00:22:18.937
"And that no boundless thirst
for surplus labor will arise

00:22:18.937 --> 00:22:22.317
"from the character of production itself.

00:22:22.317 --> 00:22:26.447
"Hence in antiquity
overwork becomes frightful

00:22:26.447 --> 00:22:30.357
"only when the aim is
to obtain exchange value

00:22:30.357 --> 00:22:33.017
"in its independent monetary shape,

00:22:33.017 --> 00:22:36.467
"i.e. in the production
of gold and silver.

00:22:36.467 --> 00:22:39.787
"The recognized form of overwork here

00:22:39.787 --> 00:22:42.447
"is forced labor until death.

00:22:42.447 --> 00:22:46.337
"Nevertheless, these are
exceptions in antiquity."

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:50.510
So there were exceptions
specifically in the context

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:52.930
of the production of mineral wealth,

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:56.230
but the normative rule, so it was thought,

00:22:56.230 --> 00:23:01.230
was that slavery used labor
power without despoiling it.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:02.840
And in this crucial sense,

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:06.200
it did not approach the abuses typical

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.360
of 19th century industrial wage labor.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.610
For instance, when
describing how the value

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:14.870
of labor power is determined

00:23:14.870 --> 00:23:17.890
by the social costs of its production,

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:20.780
Marx drew on the example
of ancient Roman slavery

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:22.490
to illustrate the point,

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:26.730
explaining that the
overseers in ancient Rome

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.380
received less food

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:32.250
than the agricultural
slaves they supervised,

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:37.250
because they expended less
labor than the enslaved workers.

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:41.300
And so in this sense,

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:46.300
19th century European political
economy understood slavery

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:50.320
as a patriarchal and
benign mode of production

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:54.500
that was inimical to the
features unique to capital,

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:58.418
its drive, as Marx called
it, to produce exchange value

00:23:58.418 --> 00:24:01.810
and the economies of scale
by which its circuits

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:06.810
of valorization renewed
themselves in limitless fashion,

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:07.923
as he put it.

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:11.180
This at least was the body

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:14.660
of theoretical assumptions Marx inherited

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:17.373
in 19th century economic thought.

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:23.220
So what did Marx need
to know about slavery

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:24.683
but failed to ask?

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:31.270
It was by exceeding the
limits it was supposed to have

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:34.510
that slavery became an economic category

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:36.793
so important to capital's story.

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:41.450
The system of slavery made
by European interventions

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:44.500
in Africa and the Americas was a novel

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:46.250
and unprecedented one.

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:49.260
And it shared with European
industrial wage labor

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:52.440
elements Marx revealed as central

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.450
to the workings of capital.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:58.470
It produced surplus value by
making labor power available

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:02.373
in the market in virtually limitless form.

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:07.000
And it did so by the
bureaucratic management

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:08.753
of market circulations.

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:15.670
Marx needed to devote
sustained analytical attention

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:19.000
to the bureaucratically
managed market circulations

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:20.940
that roped Africa

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:24.260
into Europe's transatlantic
commercial network

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:27.690
and pulled enslaved African labor power

00:25:27.690 --> 00:25:31.683
into the net of European
colonizing in the Americas.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:36.720
And he needed to reconcile
his inherited abstractions

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.070
about slavery as a mode of production

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:41.570
with the actual material history

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:44.130
of slavery in the New World.

00:25:44.130 --> 00:25:48.060
To do that, he needed to ask how exactly

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:51.330
did slavery give the colonies their value?

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:53.870
He needed to ask more precisely,

00:25:53.870 --> 00:25:57.610
how did the labor power
of enslaved Africans

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:00.393
give the colonies so much value?

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:05.340
A full account of modern
capitalism needs to be able

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:08.690
to explain slavery's transformation

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:11.683
under European colonization
in the Americas.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:17.480
But these questions
were already foreclosed

00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:21.500
by Marx's ahistorical
understandings of slavery

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:24.060
and the limited scale of his imagination

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:27.000
with regard to the
conditions of possibility

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:30.480
for the capital relation to emerge.

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:34.820
The result was the awkward
and erroneous grafting

00:26:34.820 --> 00:26:38.090
of an anachronistic theory of slavery

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:41.570
onto an historical
reality whose full scope

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:46.083
and operative logics Marx
failed to fully grasp.

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:48.623
Next slide, please.

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:53.630
So we get statements like this one

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:55.410
that produced the provocative

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:58.210
and often quoted pedestal metaphor

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:02.230
by making American
slavery the benign system

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:05.530
that 19th century
economic thought imagined

00:27:05.530 --> 00:27:08.220
all slavery systems to be,

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:13.220
until transformed by 19th
century industrialization.

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:17.320
Only then when the cotton industry emerged

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:20.210
was American slavery, so it was thought,

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:23.340
transformed into something akin

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.970
to the capital relation itself.

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:29.177
And so Marx writes,
"While the cotton industry

00:27:29.177 --> 00:27:32.207
"introduced child slavery into England,

00:27:32.207 --> 00:27:33.467
"in the United States,

00:27:33.467 --> 00:27:36.387
"it gave the impulse
for the transformation

00:27:36.387 --> 00:27:40.582
"of the earlier, more or
less patriarchal slavery

00:27:40.582 --> 00:27:44.517
"into a system of commercial exploitation.

00:27:44.517 --> 00:27:48.907
"In fact, the veiled
slavery of the wage laborers

00:27:48.907 --> 00:27:52.247
"in Europe needed the unqualified slavery

00:27:52.247 --> 00:27:56.057
"of the New World as its pedestal."

00:27:58.520 --> 00:27:59.853
Next slide, please.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:03.210
And we get this passage

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:05.150
that continues the line of discussion

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:07.610
we looked at a few minutes ago,

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.930
regarding slavery's general orientation

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:12.370
toward production of use values

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:15.580
with no boundless thirst
for surplus labor,

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:18.970
according to 19th century economic theory.

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:23.140
Marx continues the thought as follows:

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:26.390
As soon as peoples whose
production still moves

00:28:26.390 --> 00:28:29.410
within the lower forms of slave labor,

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:33.770
the courvee, et cetera, are
drawn into a world market

00:28:33.770 --> 00:28:37.170
dominated by the capitalist
mode of production,

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:39.880
whereby the sale of
their products for export

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:43.560
develops into their principal interest,

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:47.170
the civilized horrors
of overwork are grafted

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:52.170
onto the barbaric horrors of
slavery, serfdom, et cetera.

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:57.220
Hence the negro labor
in the southern states

00:28:57.220 --> 00:29:00.110
of the American Union preserved

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:02.640
a moderately patriarchal character,

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:05.890
as long as production was chiefly directed

00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:09.043
to the satisfaction of
immediate local requirements.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.230
But in proportion, as the export of cotton

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:16.450
became a vital interest to those states,

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:18.730
the overworking of the negro

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:21.400
and sometimes the consumption of his life

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:24.770
in seven years of labor became a factor

00:29:24.770 --> 00:29:28.233
and a calculated and calculating system.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:30.790
It was no longer a question

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:34.970
of obtaining from him a certain
quantity of useful products

00:29:34.970 --> 00:29:39.433
but rather of the production
of surplus value itself.

00:29:41.558 --> 00:29:45.430
So Marx recognized an affinity

00:29:45.430 --> 00:29:48.500
between capitalism and slavery.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:51.030
The one was the pedestal for the other.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:54.230
But his only recourse to
explain the relationship

00:29:54.230 --> 00:29:56.918
was the egregiously erroneous notion

00:29:56.918 --> 00:29:59.780
that there was nothing interesting to know

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:03.620
or observe about the
American slave labor system

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:08.620
until it came under the dark
cloud of European demand

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.740
for slave-grown cotton.

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:14.080
Only then did it become, in his view,

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:17.053
a calculated and calculating system.

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:21.040
We have to wonder,

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:23.880
as he conducted the research
that became "Capital,"

00:30:23.880 --> 00:30:27.340
sitting in the famed Reading
Room of the British Museum,

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:30.540
a monument to British colonialism founded

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:32.780
by 18th century naturalist

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:35.950
and plantation physician Hans Sloane,

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:39.490
did Marx imagine that
enslaved people ate better

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:42.590
than their overseers across
nearly two centuries,

00:30:42.590 --> 00:30:46.270
producing tobacco and rice and indigo

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:49.850
in the plantation societies
of English North America,

00:30:49.850 --> 00:30:52.050
the same as they purportedly did

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:55.860
in what he understood as
the benign slave system

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:57.053
of ancient Rome?

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:00.173
Next slide, please.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:07.440
There's one crucial passage in "Capital"

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:12.220
where Marx comes closest
to getting it right.

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:14.320
And it is this very long passage

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:17.810
where drawing on contemporary economist

00:31:17.810 --> 00:31:20.800
J.E. Cairnes' book "The Slave Power,"

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:24.840
Marx comes closest to a full elaboration

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.150
of slavery's transformation

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:31.653
under racial capitalism and colonialism.

00:31:32.647 --> 00:31:35.937
"The slave owner buys
his worker," he writes,

00:31:35.937 --> 00:31:39.257
"in the same way as he buys his horse.

00:31:39.257 --> 00:31:44.247
"If he loses his slave, he
loses a piece of capital,

00:31:44.247 --> 00:31:46.837
"which he must replace
by fresh expenditure

00:31:46.837 --> 00:31:48.417
"on the slave market.

00:31:48.417 --> 00:31:52.320
"But take note of this," and
now he is quoting Cairnes:

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:54.390
The rice grounds of Georgia

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:58.280
or the swamps of the Mississippi
may be fatally injurious

00:31:58.280 --> 00:32:02.490
to the human constitution,
but the waste of human life

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:07.490
which the cultivation of
these districts necessitates

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:10.670
is not so great that it cannot be repaired

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:14.690
from the teeming preserves
of Virginia and Kentucky.

00:32:14.690 --> 00:32:17.240
Considerations of economy, moreover,

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:21.210
which under a natural
system afford some security

00:32:21.210 --> 00:32:24.040
for humane treatment by identifying

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:28.097
in the master's interest
with the slave's preservation

00:32:28.097 --> 00:32:31.740
when once trading in slaves is practiced

00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:35.270
becomes reasons for
racking to the uttermost

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:37.510
the toil of the slave.

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:40.830
For when his place can at once supplied

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:42.830
from foreign preserves,

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:47.180
the duration of his life
becomes a matter of less moment

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:50.700
than its productiveness while it lasts.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:51.533
Next slide.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:58.180
It is accordingly a maxim slave management

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:00.830
in slave-importing countries

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:04.690
that the most effective
economy is that which takes out

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:08.190
of human chattel in the
shortest space of time

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:11.350
the utmost amount of
exertion it is capable

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:12.760
of putting forth.

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:14.800
It is in the tropical culture,

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:18.090
where annual profits often
equal the whole capital

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:20.470
of plantations that negro life

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:22.730
is most recklessly sacrificed.

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:25.320
It is the agriculture of the West Indies,

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:30.240
which has been for centuries
prolific of fabulous wealth

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:33.110
that has engulfed millions
of the African race.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:37.300
It is in Cuba at this day,
whose revenues are reckoned

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:41.240
by millions and whose planters are princes

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:45.780
that we see in the servile
class the coursest fare,

00:33:45.780 --> 00:33:48.430
the most exhausting and unremitting toil,

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:51.390
and even the absolute destruction

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:55.470
of a portion of its numbers every year.

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:59.857
And now Marx quotes the Roman poet Horace,

00:33:59.857 --> 00:34:04.480
"The name is changed, but
the tale is told of you,"

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:07.227
and concludes, "For the slave trade,

00:34:07.227 --> 00:34:10.267
"read or substitute labor market,

00:34:10.267 --> 00:34:13.807
"for Kentucky and Virginia
substitute Ireland

00:34:13.807 --> 00:34:15.997
"and the agricultural
districts of England,

00:34:15.997 --> 00:34:20.997
"Scotland and Wales, for
Africa substitute Germany."

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:24.903
What's missing here,

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:31.030
though it's right there beneath
Marx's eyes is theorization

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:35.390
of the slave trade
itself as a labor market.

00:34:35.390 --> 00:34:39.000
It is not just analogous to
the European labor market

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:41.970
but rather the original instantiation

00:34:41.970 --> 00:34:45.340
of a particular kind of labor market,

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:49.810
one that produced and circulated
commodified labor power

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:52.730
in the pursuit of surplus value

00:34:52.730 --> 00:34:55.593
and did so on a limitless scale.

00:34:57.210 --> 00:35:00.590
Marx's invocation of a historical process

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:04.070
that drew slavery into a world market

00:35:04.070 --> 00:35:06.540
should have been a reference

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:08.730
to the transatlantic slave trade

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:11.970
and the system of plantation
slavery it sustained

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:16.780
across the colonized
Americas, but it wasn't.

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:20.130
His account of New World
slavery as organized

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:22.240
around production of use values

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:25.940
and thus preserving a moderately
patriarchal character,

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:29.960
as he put it, until it was transformed

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:32.230
by the distorting influence of capital

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:37.230
in the 19th century is more
than a harmless anachronism.

00:35:37.480 --> 00:35:41.990
In other words, he doesn't
just get the chronology wrong.

00:35:41.990 --> 00:35:45.660
He's unable to fully perceive the nature

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:49.680
of the relationship of
slavery to capitalism.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.130
He credited New World slavery

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:55.410
with being an important economic category,

00:35:55.410 --> 00:35:58.720
but absent theorization of
slavery's transformation

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:01.050
under European colonialism,

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:05.713
he failed to perceive the
new labor form it innovated.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:11.310
So Marx ended up stuck without
a theoretical foundation

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:16.310
for organizing what he knew to
be true of New World slavery,

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:20.660
that it was organized
to produce surplus value

00:36:20.660 --> 00:36:25.290
and did so on an economy
of scale never before seen

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:26.483
in world history.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:31.940
This made it not just an
important economic category

00:36:31.940 --> 00:36:35.030
but, moreover, a mode of production

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:39.130
that begged for theorization
on the same terms

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:42.947
as those he deployed to
understand wage labor

00:36:42.947 --> 00:36:45.300
and to elaborate the formula

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:48.393
for capital's production of surplus value.

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:54.470
For lack of an ability to
theorize this transformation

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:58.070
of slavery under European
colonizing in the Americas,

00:36:58.070 --> 00:37:01.320
Marx cannot but misapprehend

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:04.680
what was its most important feature

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:07.793
with regard to the formula for capital.

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:13.760
By erasing the long history
of colonial chattel slavery

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:16.830
and the transatlantic
slave trade that fueled it,

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:21.200
Marx failed to account
for its economy of scale

00:37:21.200 --> 00:37:24.480
and how it achieved that scale,

00:37:24.480 --> 00:37:29.440
by the ongoing appearance of
African commodified labor power

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:30.863
in the marketplace.

00:37:32.390 --> 00:37:35.680
The African captive
purchased by representatives

00:37:35.680 --> 00:37:39.060
of the European state on the African coast

00:37:39.060 --> 00:37:42.700
and sold to European planter settlers

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:46.000
in the plantation colonies in the Americas

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.090
was the primordial figure

00:37:48.090 --> 00:37:51.020
of the worker whose
labor power was subjected

00:37:51.020 --> 00:37:55.160
to the violences of
capital's boundless thirst

00:37:55.160 --> 00:37:58.193
and voracious appetite for surplus labor.

00:37:59.260 --> 00:38:03.550
By the terms then of his
own theorization of capital,

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:08.170
American slavery was
organized around an iteration

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:11.943
of the capital relation
from its beginnings.

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:14.690
Next slide, please

00:38:18.809 --> 00:38:23.809
Marx could not empirically know free labor

00:38:24.065 --> 00:38:27.580
to be the singular labor form able

00:38:27.580 --> 00:38:31.810
to sustain the capital
relation's social reproduction

00:38:31.810 --> 00:38:35.590
and satisfy its hunger
for self-valorization,

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:39.490
because he did not closely examine

00:38:39.490 --> 00:38:42.910
what proceeded that innovation,

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:47.850
labor power's continual
replacement in the market

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:52.850
by the always reliable reappearance
on the Atlantic horizon

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:55.563
of the transatlantic slave ship.

00:38:56.590 --> 00:39:00.380
Colonial slavery
represents a transformation

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:04.960
antecedent to and no
less revolutionary than

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:07.420
the industrial system's appropriation

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:08.883
of free wage labor.

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:14.193
We might call this system
colonial slave capitalism.

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:20.070
Its circulations began with
money expended in Europe

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:22.940
to buy commodities to exchange for people

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:24.760
on the African coast,

00:39:24.760 --> 00:39:29.020
and value was mediated
by a transatlantic series

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:31.490
of commodity conversions,

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:35.040
European commodities
exchanged for captive people

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:37.130
on the African coast,

00:39:37.130 --> 00:39:40.120
people converted to
commodified labor power

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.880
in the transatlantic slave ships,

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:47.010
commodified labor power
consumed in the cultivation

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:51.080
of cash crops in the
American plantation systems.

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:54.260
And finally, the circulation closed

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:56.490
with money's final return

00:39:56.490 --> 00:39:59.370
back to its initial point of departure

00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:03.550
in valorized form by the sale of the sugar

00:40:03.550 --> 00:40:07.740
or tobacco or rice or indigo made

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:11.860
by the alienation of slave
labor power by coercion

00:40:11.860 --> 00:40:14.650
with all that that entailed, a violence

00:40:14.650 --> 00:40:18.403
and indifference toward the
life of the enslaved worker.

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:23.870
The economic form created
by the circulation

00:40:23.870 --> 00:40:27.390
of African people as
commodities was money,

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:30.750
and moreover, capital in the Marxian sense

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:34.670
of being money whose value was enlarged

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:38.230
by the expropriation of their labor power.

00:40:38.230 --> 00:40:42.670
In other words, the
transatlantic circulation

00:40:42.670 --> 00:40:47.210
of Africans followed
capital's general formula.

00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:51.360
It put money into circulation
to produce surplus value

00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.853
that in turn produced more money.

00:40:55.970 --> 00:40:59.100
There is no more
straightforward expression

00:40:59.100 --> 00:41:03.840
of what Marx called quote,
"the valorization of value"

00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:08.450
than what a Jamaican planter
named Cary Helyar said

00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:11.240
of the enslaved Africans he purchased

00:41:11.240 --> 00:41:15.610
with money advanced by
his brother in England.

00:41:15.610 --> 00:41:18.372
After acquiring 14 enslaved Africans

00:41:18.372 --> 00:41:22.110
out of a Dutch ship in 1642,

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:24.410
he wrote to his brother promising

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:29.070
that by their labor and the
profits that would ensue,

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:33.923
he would quote, "Make
these negroes buy more."

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:39.180
The 18th century British promoter

00:41:39.180 --> 00:41:42.470
of slavery Malachi Postlethwaite opined

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:47.380
that the Africa trade was an
inexhaustible fund of wealth

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:51.710
for the state, but it was more than that.

00:41:51.710 --> 00:41:55.460
The system it fueled was
the original laboratory

00:41:55.460 --> 00:41:59.300
in which the owners of
money first realized

00:41:59.300 --> 00:42:02.960
and innovated the
production of surplus value

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:07.300
by labor power's ongoing
alienation in the marketplace.

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:10.580
That's what Cary Helyar understood.

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:14.373
And it's what Marx evaded
in his account of capital.

00:42:16.420 --> 00:42:18.410
So moving now toward a close,

00:42:18.410 --> 00:42:20.550
what I've tried to show here today

00:42:20.550 --> 00:42:23.718
is that the colonial Atlantic slave system

00:42:23.718 --> 00:42:27.020
was a self-perpetuating one,

00:42:27.020 --> 00:42:29.640
a system of surplus value production

00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:34.470
driven by commoditization of
labor power in the marketplace.

00:42:34.470 --> 00:42:38.920
It innovated this by what
I'm calling the alienation

00:42:38.920 --> 00:42:41.330
of labor by coercion

00:42:41.330 --> 00:42:44.690
and did so prior to
the European innovation

00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:49.123
of what Marx called the
alienation of labor power by sale.

00:42:50.210 --> 00:42:55.210
And my point is not to flatten
or dissolve the distinction

00:42:55.380 --> 00:42:57.423
between these two forms.

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:00.600
What I am insisting on though

00:43:00.600 --> 00:43:04.850
is that characterizing
the colonial slave system

00:43:04.850 --> 00:43:09.490
as one whose principle role was
as an aggregate accumulation

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:11.840
of wealth that was primitive

00:43:11.840 --> 00:43:14.640
in the sense of being outside of

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:18.900
and external to
capitalism's time and place

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:21.750
in 19th century Northern Europe,

00:43:21.750 --> 00:43:25.880
Marx's primitive accumulation obscures

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:27.833
more than it reveals.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:32.010
By attributing to colonial slavery only

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:35.240
the aggregate accumulation
of startup capital,

00:43:35.240 --> 00:43:38.550
the primitive accumulation concept masks

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:42.120
and leaves uninvestigated and untheorized

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:46.140
the ways colonial slavery
produced that wealth

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:50.700
by an innovation in the
expropriation of labor power.

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.790
It erases colonial slavery's scale.

00:43:53.790 --> 00:43:56.450
It distorts its chronology,

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:59.740
and it occludes the
bureaucratic rationalization

00:43:59.740 --> 00:44:01.840
of expropriation by violence

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:05.010
developed across three centuries

00:44:05.010 --> 00:44:08.823
prior to American slavery's
turn to cotton production.

00:44:10.600 --> 00:44:13.210
To invoke Lisa Lowe's analysis

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:17.260
of what she calls the
intimacies of four continents,

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:19.860
a formulation that is so helpful

00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:22.590
for seeing relations otherwise obscured

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:25.931
by liberal economic and political thought,

00:44:25.931 --> 00:44:29.320
it obscures the ways the managed violences

00:44:29.320 --> 00:44:31.730
of colonial capitalist slavery

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:35.493
across three centuries were
not external to Europe at all.

00:44:36.741 --> 00:44:41.270
How much enslaved or
indentured labor power,

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.850
for instance, did Marx himself consume

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:48.590
in the sugar that sweetened
the tea he surely drank

00:44:48.590 --> 00:44:52.080
as he sat in the British
Museum Reading Room

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:55.383
developing his critique
of political economy?

00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:59.710
As Lisa Lowe explains by
way of a brilliant reading

00:44:59.710 --> 00:45:03.437
of the novel "Vanity Fair,"
"Thackeray's distillation

00:45:03.437 --> 00:45:06.867
"of early Victorian
British society revealed

00:45:06.867 --> 00:45:11.867
"what Marx's account of capital
did not, the intimacies,"

00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:15.367
and I'm quoting here,
"between colonial commodities

00:45:15.367 --> 00:45:19.607
"in early Victorian households
and the imperial relations

00:45:19.607 --> 00:45:22.837
"to Africa, Asia, and the Americas

00:45:22.837 --> 00:45:26.453
"to which they were
inextricably tied," end quote.

00:45:27.727 --> 00:45:30.407
"Marx's account," according to Lowe,

00:45:30.407 --> 00:45:32.907
"simultaneously recognized

00:45:32.907 --> 00:45:37.077
"and ingeniously suppressed the histories

00:45:37.077 --> 00:45:40.477
"and geographies of
production that brought sugar,

00:45:40.477 --> 00:45:44.827
"tea, silks, and so forth
to British consumers."

00:45:46.770 --> 00:45:51.110
And so if a central event
of primitive accumulation

00:45:51.110 --> 00:45:54.560
and its hastening of
the shift from feudalism

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:57.750
to capitalism was the transformation

00:45:57.750 --> 00:45:59.560
of the slave mode of production,

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:02.700
such that it could exceed the limits

00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:05.420
that traditionally inhered in the use

00:46:05.420 --> 00:46:09.620
of slave labor power, it
changes our understanding

00:46:09.620 --> 00:46:12.570
of the relationship between the two

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:14.963
in important and profound ways.

00:46:15.940 --> 00:46:19.560
It suggests that by
understanding American slavery

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:23.490
as the pedestal on which
European wage slavery stood,

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:27.560
if we want to keep Marx's
pedestal and veil metaphor,

00:46:27.560 --> 00:46:31.229
we are not just naming slave-grown cotton

00:46:31.229 --> 00:46:35.393
as the fuel that fed the system
of wage slavery in Europe,

00:46:36.240 --> 00:46:39.870
but moreover, we're naming as primordial

00:46:39.870 --> 00:46:43.980
the three centuries of
bureaucratically-managed production

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:48.420
of surplus value through
expropriation of labor power

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:50.480
in the Americas.

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:53.470
We are thus producing better understanding

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:57.350
of why slavery and capitalism
share so many features,

00:46:57.350 --> 00:47:01.412
why slave labor and wage
labor are not as distinct

00:47:01.412 --> 00:47:05.783
as liberal economic theory
would have us believe.

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:11.280
We are unmasking the otherwise
occluded transformation

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:16.203
of the slave mode of production
under European colonialism.

00:47:17.660 --> 00:47:20.600
And we're better understanding the nature

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:23.603
of the pedestal of colonial slavery.

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:28.260
Both colonial slavery

00:47:28.260 --> 00:47:31.790
and 19th century industrial production

00:47:31.790 --> 00:47:35.910
achieved economies of scale
and limitless capacity

00:47:35.910 --> 00:47:38.970
from money's perpetual self-valorization

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:41.190
by the managed bureaucracy

00:47:41.190 --> 00:47:43.603
of labor's alienation in the market.

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:47.350
We must not collapse the distinction

00:47:47.350 --> 00:47:50.120
between the alienation
of labor by coercion

00:47:51.160 --> 00:47:53.233
and its alienation by sale.

00:47:54.300 --> 00:47:57.670
But in so far as the
one proceeded the other

00:47:57.670 --> 00:48:01.180
and represents a primordial
innovation realized

00:48:01.180 --> 00:48:04.440
by slavery's transformation
under colonialism,

00:48:04.440 --> 00:48:08.118
we can usefully rethink
the way we periodize

00:48:08.118 --> 00:48:10.860
and organize our understanding

00:48:10.860 --> 00:48:13.493
of capital's long chronology.

00:48:14.510 --> 00:48:16.920
It's in this sense that I insist

00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:20.090
that colonial slavery must be understood

00:48:20.090 --> 00:48:23.060
as more central even than Marx recognized

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:25.240
with his provocative metaphors

00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:29.830
that name the proximity
of slavery to capitalism

00:48:29.830 --> 00:48:34.830
without adequately grasping the
nature of that relationship.

00:48:34.850 --> 00:48:37.940
And in this sense, to remind us

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:40.410
of the genealogy of
black radical thinkers,

00:48:40.410 --> 00:48:43.500
including W. E. B. Du Bois, C. L. R. James

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:44.940
and Eric Williams

00:48:44.940 --> 00:48:48.820
who've made the same
point in various ways,

00:48:48.820 --> 00:48:53.400
I'm especially indebted to
the sociologist Oliver Cox

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:58.400
who suggested in his 1964
work "Capitalism as a System"

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:02.827
that quote, "Marx begins
his analysis of the nature

00:49:02.827 --> 00:49:07.337
"of capitalism almost where
he might have ended it.

00:49:07.337 --> 00:49:11.257
"And as is commonly the
case in classical economics,

00:49:11.257 --> 00:49:15.397
"he relegates as
subsidiary the very things

00:49:15.397 --> 00:49:19.667
"which should have been
the center of his study.

00:49:19.667 --> 00:49:23.187
"His primitive accumulation is none other

00:49:23.187 --> 00:49:27.943
"than fundamentally capitalist
accumulation," end quote.

00:49:31.690 --> 00:49:35.850
Across three centuries,
capital realized the means

00:49:35.850 --> 00:49:39.510
to satisfy its hunger for valorization

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:43.660
by the consumption of some
12 and a half million souls,

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:46.470
Well before it shifted to the scene

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:49.660
elaborated so painstakingly by Marx,

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:52.950
the labor market where
the wage worker appears

00:49:52.950 --> 00:49:55.300
under the ruse that is bourgeois freedom,

00:49:55.300 --> 00:49:59.533
but is, in fact, the end
line of his own enslavement.

00:50:01.210 --> 00:50:03.930
If we can understand capital's alienation

00:50:03.930 --> 00:50:07.780
of the wage worker's
extensively free labor power

00:50:07.780 --> 00:50:11.540
as further innovation of the
proceeding three centuries

00:50:11.540 --> 00:50:14.570
of alienation by direct coercion,

00:50:14.570 --> 00:50:18.620
as much as being a revolutionary
break with that past,

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:22.580
we have the means to better
understand the intimacies

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:25.070
of slavery and capitalism

00:50:25.070 --> 00:50:28.670
and the importance of
slavery's transformation

00:50:28.670 --> 00:50:31.880
under racial settler colonialism,

00:50:31.880 --> 00:50:36.880
not just to modern capitalism's
conditions of possibility

00:50:38.150 --> 00:50:42.420
but also to its core
technologies of violences,

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:45.090
both material and social,

00:50:45.090 --> 00:50:49.110
that shape our worlds to this day.

00:50:49.110 --> 00:50:49.943
Thank you.

00:50:52.220 --> 00:50:54.070
- Thank you so much.

00:50:54.070 --> 00:50:56.468
This is where there would
be thunderous applause,

00:50:56.468 --> 00:51:01.468
and we just are imagining it this moment.

00:51:01.800 --> 00:51:06.750
Thank you for a great and
eye-opening presentation.

00:51:06.750 --> 00:51:09.670
We're going to open
the floor to questions,

00:51:09.670 --> 00:51:12.190
and I'm gonna ask folks to put
their questions in the chat.

00:51:12.190 --> 00:51:13.500
I especially invite students

00:51:13.500 --> 00:51:16.240
from the seminar to pose questions.

00:51:16.240 --> 00:51:19.420
Maybe I'll start with one, if
that's okay, if you're ready.

00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:20.793
- [Dr. Smallwood] Yeah, yeah.

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:25.340
- So, you know,

00:51:25.340 --> 00:51:29.040
I find it totally convincing

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:33.292
and I wonder what other
steps we could take

00:51:33.292 --> 00:51:38.292
in thinking about this system,

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:40.620
the system before the system,

00:51:40.620 --> 00:51:43.663
the first system, the
model prototype system,

00:51:45.100 --> 00:51:50.100
and so one thing that happens
in the wage labor system

00:51:50.130 --> 00:51:52.370
that Marx spends a long time
talking about in "Capital"

00:51:52.370 --> 00:51:55.430
is the pressure to increase productivity

00:51:55.430 --> 00:51:57.563
and to expand the system.

00:51:58.670 --> 00:52:02.109
And some of that has to do
with encountering limits

00:52:02.109 --> 00:52:07.109
to the number of wage
laborers that you can get.

00:52:07.170 --> 00:52:11.450
But there seems to be a kind
of motor of growth in it.

00:52:11.450 --> 00:52:13.280
And I wonder if you see anything like that

00:52:13.280 --> 00:52:15.910
in the slave capital
system that proceeded it

00:52:15.910 --> 00:52:17.650
that you've been describing,

00:52:17.650 --> 00:52:20.270
growth, how technology fits,

00:52:20.270 --> 00:52:21.630
is there any pressure

00:52:21.630 --> 00:52:26.630
to make the slave trade obsolete,

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:31.490
to diminish it in those ways

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:33.713
to help the growth of surplus value,

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:37.533
or is growth not that big of a factor?

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:41.370
- Oh, I would say growth is a huge factor.

00:52:41.370 --> 00:52:43.710
And I think, you know, part
of what I think happens is

00:52:43.710 --> 00:52:48.710
that we tend to view these
things teleologically.

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:50.973
So, you know, again, of course,

00:52:52.120 --> 00:52:54.590
wage labor under industrialization

00:52:54.590 --> 00:52:56.760
innovates something even further.

00:52:56.760 --> 00:52:59.670
But if we're looking at the
slave system on its own terms

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:02.040
instead of looking at it, you
know, already comparing it

00:53:02.040 --> 00:53:05.334
to what is going to happen
a hundred years later,

00:53:05.334 --> 00:53:08.130
it's an inefficient system compared

00:53:08.130 --> 00:53:10.457
to what's gonna develop,

00:53:10.457 --> 00:53:11.880
but it's a system

00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:14.453
in which people are
desperately trying to innovate.

00:53:15.389 --> 00:53:17.410
And so, you know, some of the studies,

00:53:17.410 --> 00:53:20.520
for instance, of plantation
slavery in the sugar colonies

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:23.590
in the Caribbean has shown
that there's an increase

00:53:23.590 --> 00:53:26.990
in productivity that
can only be explained by

00:53:26.990 --> 00:53:29.846
the further ramping up of slave labor.

00:53:29.846 --> 00:53:34.290
And I think part of what
I'm trying to do here

00:53:34.290 --> 00:53:37.660
and one of the other things
I think we struggle with is,

00:53:37.660 --> 00:53:40.080
the only measure we have in many ways

00:53:40.080 --> 00:53:45.080
of the immensity of that system
is the quantitative history,

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:47.610
you know, the numbers.

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:51.280
And it's just so easy to
let 12 and a half million

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:52.643
become an abstraction.

00:53:53.617 --> 00:53:56.540
It's so hard for us to really visualize

00:53:56.540 --> 00:54:01.403
how much that is the social
reproduction of slavery.

00:54:02.525 --> 00:54:07.525
This maxim of using the slave labor up,

00:54:07.790 --> 00:54:09.600
knowing that you can replace them,

00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:11.400
that it is more efficient.

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:15.736
So again, this idea
that slavery can't work,

00:54:15.736 --> 00:54:19.430
it shouldn't work, but it did.

00:54:19.430 --> 00:54:21.570
And so that's, I think,
what we have to reckon with

00:54:21.570 --> 00:54:24.210
is just how much they were able

00:54:24.210 --> 00:54:25.530
and willing to make it work

00:54:25.530 --> 00:54:27.601
and that they did so

00:54:27.601 --> 00:54:31.350
through a bureaucratic
means of management.

00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:33.500
I think part of what Marx
is always trying to do

00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:36.030
is to make this primitive accumulation,

00:54:36.030 --> 00:54:39.780
because it's happening,
you know, extrajudicially

00:54:39.780 --> 00:54:42.320
and outside the state and
it's this kind of domain

00:54:42.320 --> 00:54:44.110
of the wild and the piratical,

00:54:44.110 --> 00:54:46.523
and no, it's the state.

00:54:49.183 --> 00:54:52.730
And the archive, the colonial archive,

00:54:52.730 --> 00:54:54.660
the colonial state archive

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:57.330
that documents it is going to make its way

00:54:57.330 --> 00:54:58.570
to the British Library.

00:54:58.570 --> 00:55:02.440
If he were researching capital,
probably would've needed

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:05.233
to be maybe 50 years later.

00:55:06.560 --> 00:55:08.800
Instead of pouring over the Blue Books

00:55:08.800 --> 00:55:10.927
he could have poured over

00:55:10.927 --> 00:55:13.523
the Royal African Company's papers.

00:55:17.910 --> 00:55:19.000
- Thanks, yes.

00:55:19.000 --> 00:55:20.993
That would have changed a lot.

00:55:21.910 --> 00:55:25.540
One hopes, and okay.

00:55:25.540 --> 00:55:28.830
I'm not seeing questions
in the chat so much.

00:55:28.830 --> 00:55:31.970
So if you want to raise some
issues or make some comments,

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:34.300
please feel free to do it.

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:36.073
I can, of course, keep asking.

00:55:44.549 --> 00:55:46.140
- (laughs) I'm looking at some

00:55:46.140 --> 00:55:47.457
of the comments.

00:55:55.740 --> 00:55:59.870
I think what happens
sometimes with such an opening

00:55:59.870 --> 00:56:03.630
of a whole new world is that
you can't ask the usual,

00:56:03.630 --> 00:56:05.960
you know, Marx people like to debate

00:56:05.960 --> 00:56:07.573
the finest technical points.

00:56:09.150 --> 00:56:11.520
Very rarely interesting,

00:56:11.520 --> 00:56:15.380
but this is a whole new issue

00:56:17.690 --> 00:56:19.153
from the Marx side obviously.

00:56:20.480 --> 00:56:21.887
- Question, "Could you say a little more

00:56:21.887 --> 00:56:24.187
"about the role of
bureaucratic management?

00:56:24.187 --> 00:56:28.227
"It sounds like, as a way
that the state managed slavery

00:56:28.227 --> 00:56:31.287
"as labor, it is maybe
parallel or a precursor

00:56:31.287 --> 00:56:32.857
"to the juridical equality

00:56:32.857 --> 00:56:36.147
"that, as you point out, Marx emphasized."

00:56:38.320 --> 00:56:41.046
I'm not sure what more
to say, but I guess,

00:56:41.046 --> 00:56:46.046
I'll just say that what I'm
trying to emphasize, again,

00:56:46.050 --> 00:56:51.050
is that it seems to me part
of what Marx wants to do

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:54.560
is to imagine the slave system

00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:58.540
and the violences of colonialism generally

00:56:59.537 --> 00:57:04.537
as not really being the arm of the state.

00:57:06.730 --> 00:57:11.120
Especially in the early modern period.

00:57:11.120 --> 00:57:16.120
And so I think that part
of what struck me even

00:57:16.910 --> 00:57:19.810
as a researcher when I started
studying the slave trade,

00:57:19.810 --> 00:57:24.670
again, was just to realize
just how bureaucratic

00:57:24.670 --> 00:57:26.540
and rationalized it was.

00:57:26.540 --> 00:57:30.820
And I think when we try to make sense

00:57:30.820 --> 00:57:33.580
of certain kinds of violence as, again,

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:38.580
being outside the domain
of the bureaucratic state,

00:57:40.440 --> 00:57:44.597
this kind of wild space
that's not managed,

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:52.220
I think that's a way that
we try to rationalize it.

00:57:52.220 --> 00:57:54.470
And I think what we have
to reckon with, again,

00:57:54.470 --> 00:57:59.470
is the kinds of violence that
the state was quite happy

00:58:00.810 --> 00:58:05.810
and dependent upon managing
in a very rationalizing way.

00:58:10.600 --> 00:58:15.580
- We have another question
from Sara-Maria Sorentino.

00:58:15.580 --> 00:58:19.860
Maybe we'll open up Sara-Maria's mic,

00:58:19.860 --> 00:58:21.923
and they can ask the question themselves.

00:58:23.934 --> 00:58:26.240
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:58:26.240 --> 00:58:27.073
- Thanks for the talk.

00:58:27.073 --> 00:58:28.500
I was wondering if you
could say a little bit more

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:31.244
about how it would be possible

00:58:31.244 --> 00:58:34.950
to understand or think
slavery on its own terms.

00:58:34.950 --> 00:58:36.680
Like, do you think that
there are particular ways

00:58:36.680 --> 00:58:38.720
in which we understand the critique

00:58:38.720 --> 00:58:41.190
of political economy and
understand capitalism?

00:58:41.190 --> 00:58:44.560
Is it totality and understand
materialism is a kind of form

00:58:44.560 --> 00:58:47.110
of metaphysics that might preclude us

00:58:47.110 --> 00:58:49.950
from understanding
slavery on its own terms?

00:58:49.950 --> 00:58:51.890
Like what kind of conceptual

00:58:51.890 --> 00:58:55.010
and political tools might we need

00:58:55.010 --> 00:58:57.933
in order to begin that endeavor?

00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:07.580
- I guess I don't have a
really deeply formulated answer

00:59:07.590 --> 00:59:08.423
to that question.

00:59:08.423 --> 00:59:09.433
I mean, I think,

00:59:10.690 --> 00:59:13.740
and I'm being told that my
internet connection is unstable.

00:59:13.740 --> 00:59:15.480
Can you guys hear me?

00:59:15.480 --> 00:59:17.330
- We're getting you pretty clearly, yep.

00:59:17.330 --> 00:59:22.330
- I think the way to see it
on its own terms is simply to,

00:59:27.410 --> 00:59:31.730
to just, you know, use the
archival material that we have

00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:35.780
and be critically reflective

00:59:35.780 --> 00:59:39.680
such that we're not
projecting teleologically

00:59:44.300 --> 00:59:47.154
other formations onto the slave system.

00:59:47.154 --> 00:59:48.300
I don't know if that makes sense,

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:52.840
but I don't have a
really sophisticated way

00:59:54.700 --> 00:59:57.463
to say what I mean by that, I don't think.

00:59:58.330 --> 00:59:59.190
- Yeah, I mean,

00:59:59.190 --> 01:00:01.590
this is something that
I study and research.

01:00:01.590 --> 01:00:05.850
And so if part of like our
understanding of ideology

01:00:05.850 --> 01:00:08.760
or the way that capitalism
impacts our consciousness

01:00:08.760 --> 01:00:12.530
is to make it so that
we see certain versions

01:00:12.530 --> 01:00:15.384
of freedom as the freedom
that we need to pursue,

01:00:15.384 --> 01:00:16.257
I guess I'm-

01:00:16.257 --> 01:00:17.870
- I'm not hearing you.

01:00:17.870 --> 01:00:18.703
- Oh, sorry.

01:00:18.703 --> 01:00:19.940
I'm interested, I guess,

01:00:19.940 --> 01:00:24.940
in to what extent this
problematic that you've posed,

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:27.930
in terms of ways that Marx was unable

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:32.930
to think capitalism has impacted
our critical consciousness,

01:00:33.360 --> 01:00:38.360
whether we can just approach
the archive as such,

01:00:38.450 --> 01:00:40.340
or whether there's a particular way

01:00:40.340 --> 01:00:42.060
in which the framing of capitalism

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:45.440
as the problem has actually
inhibited our critical capacity

01:00:45.440 --> 01:00:47.663
for reflection?

01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:53.860
- Well, I think that as I'm
listening to your question,

01:00:53.860 --> 01:00:58.540
I'm also reminded that the series here

01:00:58.540 --> 01:01:02.440
is the value of "Capital."

01:01:02.440 --> 01:01:06.730
And I hope my, you know,
kind of taking Marx to task

01:01:06.730 --> 01:01:08.990
doesn't imply that I
don't value "Capital,"

01:01:08.990 --> 01:01:11.680
because I value it enormously.

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:14.860
And I think one of the
things that Marx's critique

01:01:14.860 --> 01:01:18.850
of political economy does is help us

01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:23.850
to take apart the otherwise
very, very opaque,

01:01:26.370 --> 01:01:28.600
liberal, philosophical assumptions

01:01:28.600 --> 01:01:33.280
that guide so much of Western analysis.

01:01:36.270 --> 01:01:40.190
And so I think that's
a big part of, I think,

01:01:40.190 --> 01:01:42.120
what we need to do in order

01:01:47.347 --> 01:01:51.100
to sort of undo some of the assumptions

01:01:51.100 --> 01:01:53.650
that guide how we
understand these systems.

01:01:53.650 --> 01:01:55.020
I mean, for instance,

01:01:55.020 --> 01:01:59.280
one of the reasons Marx's
critique is so compelling to me

01:01:59.280 --> 01:02:03.320
and one I want to try to use
to understand colonial slavery

01:02:03.320 --> 01:02:06.050
is because a lot of the
mainstream historiography

01:02:06.050 --> 01:02:10.880
is very happy to figure
the Atlantic slavery system

01:02:10.880 --> 01:02:14.320
as nothing more or less
than the continuation

01:02:15.380 --> 01:02:18.260
of late medieval systems, you know,

01:02:18.260 --> 01:02:19.560
already in existence.

01:02:19.560 --> 01:02:22.930
And so there's a failure to
really reckon with the way

01:02:22.930 --> 01:02:27.930
that colonial slavery was a
revolutionary transformation

01:02:28.290 --> 01:02:31.750
of late medieval and earlier systems.

01:02:31.750 --> 01:02:35.893
And I think Marx's analysis
can help us do that,

01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:39.927
with some corrections for some

01:02:39.927 --> 01:02:42.873
of the things, the
blinders, that Marx had.

01:02:45.240 --> 01:02:46.520
- You have some other questions.

01:02:46.520 --> 01:02:49.660
I wanna just throw one in, in the middle,

01:02:49.660 --> 01:02:51.180
'cause you're on the topic.

01:02:51.180 --> 01:02:55.860
That is that I see that you
want to eliminate this other,

01:02:55.860 --> 01:02:58.580
really other system complete in itself

01:02:58.580 --> 01:03:01.210
without comparing it
constantly to capital,

01:03:01.210 --> 01:03:04.040
but it does seem since
it's the first innovation

01:03:04.040 --> 01:03:05.990
or it's going on simultaneously

01:03:05.990 --> 01:03:08.750
with things that are going on in Europe,

01:03:08.750 --> 01:03:11.100
in other realms,

01:03:11.100 --> 01:03:14.880
where you see the influences going there

01:03:15.900 --> 01:03:18.806
from this system of bookkeeping,

01:03:18.806 --> 01:03:20.820
this idea of surplus value

01:03:21.760 --> 01:03:24.910
and how it might have,
even if it's speculative

01:03:24.910 --> 01:03:28.920
I'm curious, influenced this
other invention, Europe?

01:03:30.770 --> 01:03:32.620
- Yeah, and I'm curious too.

01:03:32.620 --> 01:03:34.720
I mean, I think that's what we all need

01:03:34.720 --> 01:03:36.050
to be trying to figure out.

01:03:36.050 --> 01:03:38.010
And I think it's not gonna be something

01:03:38.010 --> 01:03:40.580
that we're gonna figure out necessarily

01:03:40.580 --> 01:03:45.580
through the mode of, you
know, empirical research.

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:50.790
I mean, I wonder what are
the structures of feeling?

01:03:50.790 --> 01:03:53.923
What are the things that
are embedded in a culture?

01:03:55.530 --> 01:03:57.640
And again, that's where
I think it really matters

01:03:57.640 --> 01:04:01.360
to realize that three centuries

01:04:01.360 --> 01:04:05.990
of realizing the capacity
to produce surplus labor

01:04:05.990 --> 01:04:10.990
through the alienation of labor power,

01:04:13.970 --> 01:04:18.830
how does that not set the stage

01:04:19.810 --> 01:04:22.840
for the subsequent revolutionary shift

01:04:22.840 --> 01:04:24.900
that Marx is so keen to track?

01:04:24.900 --> 01:04:28.810
And again, you know, part of
what I want to make clear,

01:04:28.810 --> 01:04:30.330
and I probably didn't in the talk,

01:04:30.330 --> 01:04:32.660
is that I'm actually less frustrated

01:04:32.660 --> 01:04:36.200
that Marx didn't do the things
he could and should have done

01:04:37.340 --> 01:04:40.210
because I don't think it's
really that mysterious

01:04:40.210 --> 01:04:44.390
that Marx wanted to sort of confine things

01:04:44.390 --> 01:04:45.240
the way that he did.

01:04:45.240 --> 01:04:46.880
I mean, he had some very real goals.

01:04:46.880 --> 01:04:51.880
He really was trying to help
stoke the revolutionary shift

01:04:52.080 --> 01:04:54.190
in his 19th century moment.

01:04:54.190 --> 01:04:57.256
So it's less really that
I'm frustrated with Marx,

01:04:57.256 --> 01:05:01.240
but that I'm frustrated
with Marxism, right.

01:05:01.240 --> 01:05:05.990
For not going ahead and
asking the questions

01:05:05.990 --> 01:05:07.570
that Marx didn't ask.

01:05:07.570 --> 01:05:09.840
Because what's so interesting
is how much he did,

01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:10.673
but he didn't.

01:05:10.673 --> 01:05:12.990
And that's why I really like, you know,

01:05:12.990 --> 01:05:17.990
Lisa's way of of showing us
how he sees but doesn't see.

01:05:19.630 --> 01:05:23.180
So much of the analysis
that I'm looking for

01:05:23.180 --> 01:05:25.230
is there in "Capital."

01:05:25.230 --> 01:05:28.300
You always think he's right
about to really go there.

01:05:28.300 --> 01:05:30.083
So again, he knows.

01:05:31.540 --> 01:05:33.593
On some level, he does know.

01:05:34.820 --> 01:05:35.653
I think.

01:05:37.890 --> 01:05:38.730
- I'm with you.

01:05:38.730 --> 01:05:41.400
All right, let's come to some
more questions in the chat.

01:05:41.400 --> 01:05:45.230
If you don't mind, I will open,

01:05:45.230 --> 01:05:48.203
ask to open the mic of Florie Seery.

01:05:49.390 --> 01:05:51.117
Short but interesting question,

01:05:51.117 --> 01:05:53.083
if you will say yours.

01:05:56.520 --> 01:05:57.870
- [Florie] Sure. Thank you.

01:06:00.035 --> 01:06:02.450
There are a million things
wrong with slavery obviously,

01:06:02.450 --> 01:06:06.190
but it seems, could you
speak about how it really

01:06:08.050 --> 01:06:11.400
in my estimation interfered with any kind

01:06:11.400 --> 01:06:13.963
of innovation in agriculture?

01:06:15.700 --> 01:06:17.500
- I'm sorry. Can you say that again?

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:19.990
- [Florie] Can you opine a bit

01:06:19.990 --> 01:06:24.990
about how slavery interfered
with innovation in agriculture?

01:06:28.733 --> 01:06:29.566
- Hmm.

01:06:32.330 --> 01:06:34.580
I mean, I'm not sure I
would say that it did.

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:42.950
Plantation managers were always trying

01:06:42.950 --> 01:06:47.950
to figure out new ways to make
the system more productive,

01:06:48.000 --> 01:06:52.710
experimenting with different cultigens

01:06:52.710 --> 01:06:54.640
of whatever the crop was

01:06:54.640 --> 01:06:59.283
that they were making their
enslaved workers produce.

01:07:01.840 --> 01:07:03.160
- [Florie] Was there not,

01:07:03.160 --> 01:07:04.560
because there was such a reliance

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:07.450
on this free abusive labor,

01:07:07.450 --> 01:07:09.400
did that not impede the idea

01:07:09.400 --> 01:07:13.750
of thinking more creatively
about systems to produce

01:07:13.750 --> 01:07:15.600
in a way that wouldn't be so harmful?

01:07:22.170 --> 01:07:25.160
- I don't, you know, I
mean, I think it's relative.

01:07:25.160 --> 01:07:27.530
I guess I'm hesitating,

01:07:27.530 --> 01:07:30.210
because there's such a powerful sense,

01:07:30.210 --> 01:07:31.850
and you get this in Marx,

01:07:31.850 --> 01:07:33.230
I mean, he has a whole passage

01:07:33.230 --> 01:07:34.580
about this somewhere in "Capital,"

01:07:34.580 --> 01:07:36.330
I'm forgetting exactly where.

01:07:36.330 --> 01:07:37.770
This notion, again,

01:07:37.770 --> 01:07:42.770
this was such a powerful
idea in the 19th century

01:07:43.260 --> 01:07:45.973
that slavery was inefficient.

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:51.980
Again, of course,
comparatively speaking, it was.

01:07:51.980 --> 01:07:54.180
But again, I don't know that it's helpful

01:07:54.180 --> 01:07:59.180
to evaluate the thinking
of plantation owners

01:08:03.320 --> 01:08:06.940
relative to what was going to come

01:08:06.940 --> 01:08:09.370
with the kinds of innovations

01:08:09.370 --> 01:08:13.090
around productivity that
shaped industrial capitalism.

01:08:13.090 --> 01:08:15.910
I mean, I think if you
were to ask the planters,

01:08:15.910 --> 01:08:20.809
they understood themselves to
be people who were interested

01:08:20.809 --> 01:08:23.010
in doing things better.

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:24.480
I mean, there was a whole genre

01:08:24.480 --> 01:08:26.950
of, you know, plantation
management literature

01:08:26.950 --> 01:08:28.930
that was about this very thing

01:08:28.930 --> 01:08:32.530
within the limits of what was available

01:08:32.530 --> 01:08:36.200
in terms of scientific
thought, in terms of technology

01:08:37.140 --> 01:08:40.660
in the 16th, 17th, and 18th centuries,

01:08:40.660 --> 01:08:43.050
particularly the 18th century

01:08:43.050 --> 01:08:45.393
and into the early 19th century.

01:08:46.860 --> 01:08:49.370
- One thing I was taking from your talk

01:08:49.370 --> 01:08:53.620
and your argument is that
the strategies of management

01:08:53.620 --> 01:08:57.680
in the slave trade system
itself were innovations.

01:08:57.680 --> 01:09:00.300
These are technologies,
technologies of control,

01:09:00.300 --> 01:09:02.750
of movement of human beings,

01:09:02.750 --> 01:09:05.078
of conversion of human
beings into commodities,

01:09:05.078 --> 01:09:08.724
which you explained so eloquently happened

01:09:08.724 --> 01:09:11.290
going across the Atlantic.

01:09:11.290 --> 01:09:15.030
Those are technologies, even
if they're not machines.

01:09:15.030 --> 01:09:18.480
- Yeah, I mean things like the Dolben Act.

01:09:18.480 --> 01:09:21.530
The Dolben Act in 1788 where they said,

01:09:21.530 --> 01:09:23.060
well, you know what?

01:09:23.060 --> 01:09:28.060
Yeah, we've decided that
overcrowding is causing more deaths

01:09:29.760 --> 01:09:30.780
in the Middle Passage.

01:09:30.780 --> 01:09:32.330
So here's how we're gonna manage it.

01:09:32.330 --> 01:09:37.330
You know, we're gonna calculate
just how much square footage

01:09:39.120 --> 01:09:44.120
per body makes death acceptable.

01:09:44.340 --> 01:09:47.750
You know, gives us a quotient of mortality

01:09:47.750 --> 01:09:49.023
that is acceptable.

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:53.973
That was late 18th century innovation.

01:09:56.800 --> 01:09:58.560
- And it's a beautiful parallel.

01:09:58.560 --> 01:10:00.170
I hate to keep saying this.

01:10:00.170 --> 01:10:02.810
Not beautiful, but it is a
parallel to what Marx says

01:10:02.810 --> 01:10:07.810
about wage labor which is
it's this peculiar commodity

01:10:07.980 --> 01:10:10.217
that gets shaped and pushed around,

01:10:10.217 --> 01:10:15.217
and the technologies of
life are intimately bound up

01:10:15.520 --> 01:10:17.833
with the production of surplus value.

01:10:20.500 --> 01:10:22.930
Can we go, I'm gonna pick
and choose a little bit,

01:10:22.930 --> 01:10:24.040
'cause some of them are not,

01:10:24.040 --> 01:10:27.060
some of the questions
are not exactly on topic,

01:10:27.060 --> 01:10:28.700
but there is a question
of the relationship

01:10:28.700 --> 01:10:32.710
of this system to systems of
indigenous and slave labor.

01:10:32.710 --> 01:10:37.710
So let's ask Sebastian
Lopez to ask their question.

01:10:40.240 --> 01:10:43.120
Thanking Audrey for finding
these names in the list

01:10:43.120 --> 01:10:48.120
and unmuting them in this total
mess that we're living in.

01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:50.900
Thank you, Audrey.

01:10:50.900 --> 01:10:55.170
- [Sebastian] Hi, thank you for the talk.

01:10:55.170 --> 01:10:56.360
It's so generative,

01:10:56.360 --> 01:11:01.280
And I feel like there's many
different theoretical points

01:11:01.280 --> 01:11:05.890
that I think open up perspective

01:11:07.020 --> 01:11:11.600
on how many different formations
were happening constantly

01:11:11.600 --> 01:11:13.490
in what we call capital at the moment.

01:11:13.490 --> 01:11:14.893
And so I was wondering,

01:11:15.910 --> 01:11:17.890
even though you weren't
talking about this,

01:11:17.890 --> 01:11:21.410
but in gesturing to the
multiplicity of formations

01:11:21.410 --> 01:11:22.393
at this moment,

01:11:23.880 --> 01:11:28.880
if you could elaborate a little
on how you see the relation

01:11:29.140 --> 01:11:34.140
between Atlantic slave
labor and, for example,

01:11:35.550 --> 01:11:40.550
the different forms of slave
labor that were happening

01:11:40.700 --> 01:11:42.650
for indigenous peoples in the Americas?

01:11:42.650 --> 01:11:44.582
And I'm thinking, for example,

01:11:44.582 --> 01:11:46.940
there's this particular narrative

01:11:46.940 --> 01:11:48.870
that the encomienda was abolished

01:11:48.870 --> 01:11:51.849
and then kind of like
indigenous labor disappeared,

01:11:51.849 --> 01:11:54.770
but then that transmitted
into different forms

01:11:54.770 --> 01:11:55.990
of forced labor.

01:11:55.990 --> 01:12:00.990
And so I'm thinking of like
(speaking in foreign language)

01:12:02.480 --> 01:12:03.991
and stuff like that.

01:12:03.991 --> 01:12:07.491
And so the question is coming out of like,

01:12:10.000 --> 01:12:13.337
how do we understand
the land dispossession

01:12:13.337 --> 01:12:17.160
and slave labor and
also the different forms

01:12:17.160 --> 01:12:21.030
in which indigenous land's
labor as well changed?

01:12:21.030 --> 01:12:24.570
And how, yeah, kind of
like something like that.

01:12:24.570 --> 01:12:26.270
- Yeah, yeah, no, that's a great question.

01:12:26.270 --> 01:12:27.690
Thank you.

01:12:27.690 --> 01:12:32.547
Well, one of the reasons
I occasionally on purpose

01:12:34.210 --> 01:12:38.640
in the talk referred not
just to colonialism generally

01:12:38.640 --> 01:12:43.120
but more specifically to
racial settler colonialism

01:12:43.120 --> 01:12:46.200
was in fact to try to signify

01:12:47.115 --> 01:12:52.115
how much the system
that we're referring to

01:12:54.870 --> 01:12:57.240
really, really, really
should be understood,

01:12:57.240 --> 01:12:59.060
has to be understood

01:12:59.060 --> 01:13:02.510
as a system that used, as I always put it,

01:13:02.510 --> 01:13:04.580
slavery and indigenous dispossession

01:13:04.580 --> 01:13:07.980
as twin tools of colonizing.

01:13:07.980 --> 01:13:12.330
And that's something that
I'd say is a leading edge

01:13:12.330 --> 01:13:14.797
of new work.

01:13:14.797 --> 01:13:17.950
I think it's a wonderful
turn that's happening

01:13:17.950 --> 01:13:21.410
in scholarship on the early Americas

01:13:21.410 --> 01:13:26.070
to recognize the intertwined stories

01:13:26.070 --> 01:13:30.820
of African and indigenous slavery

01:13:30.820 --> 01:13:35.620
and of indigenous
territorial dispossession

01:13:35.620 --> 01:13:40.620
and colonizing and slavery that
accompanies that colonizing.

01:13:41.240 --> 01:13:44.960
So we know so much more now

01:13:44.960 --> 01:13:48.020
because of the research that's starting

01:13:48.020 --> 01:13:51.800
to turn into publications
in the past 10 years or so

01:13:51.800 --> 01:13:56.800
about how prolific indigenous slavery was

01:13:58.280 --> 01:14:03.280
and how much, even as in
some parts of the Americas,

01:14:04.000 --> 01:14:06.210
indigenous slavery gave way

01:14:06.210 --> 01:14:11.210
to other systems of
exploitation like encomienda,

01:14:11.249 --> 01:14:12.520
repartimiento,

01:14:12.520 --> 01:14:15.810
that they are still systems

01:14:15.810 --> 01:14:19.610
that completely disrupt indigenous systems

01:14:19.610 --> 01:14:23.560
of rule, of dominion, of sovereignty,

01:14:23.560 --> 01:14:27.403
and they're of a piece
with African slavery,

01:14:28.750 --> 01:14:31.370
they're of a piece
alongside African slavery

01:14:32.276 --> 01:14:35.453
with what colonizing entailed.

01:14:37.990 --> 01:14:40.290
I hope that answers the
question a little bit.

01:14:41.740 --> 01:14:45.350
- Okay, let's go to some more questions,

01:14:45.350 --> 01:14:46.920
if everyone has energy.

01:14:46.920 --> 01:14:48.220
We're all low energy,

01:14:48.220 --> 01:14:50.810
because it's been such
an exhausting 24 hours.

01:14:50.810 --> 01:14:53.010
So we'll just admit that.

01:14:53.010 --> 01:14:53.880
But we're going to keep going

01:14:53.880 --> 01:14:58.130
'cause this is of lasting importance.

01:14:58.130 --> 01:14:59.785
There's some technical questions

01:14:59.785 --> 01:15:01.963
about some Marxian categories.

01:15:06.320 --> 01:15:09.674
- Yeah, well, what I would say to that,

01:15:09.674 --> 01:15:10.507
and thank you for that question.

01:15:10.507 --> 01:15:13.440
What I would say to that
is that is why I tried

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:16.210
to make it clear that
I'm certainly not trying

01:15:16.210 --> 01:15:20.420
to collapse the distinction

01:15:20.420 --> 01:15:24.113
between the two labor forms,
because they were different.

01:15:25.810 --> 01:15:27.110
But I am insisting

01:15:27.110 --> 01:15:30.770
that the initial one

01:15:31.910 --> 01:15:35.500
involving colonial slavery needs

01:15:35.500 --> 01:15:37.920
to be more squarely centered

01:15:39.010 --> 01:15:41.410
in the story of capital

01:15:41.410 --> 01:15:44.373
than Marx is able or willing to do.

01:15:46.370 --> 01:15:49.070
And on the question of
social reproduction,

01:15:49.070 --> 01:15:51.090
again, that's where the need

01:15:52.148 --> 01:15:55.467
to really come to grips

01:15:57.470 --> 01:16:00.020
with the scale of the slave
trade comes into play,

01:16:00.020 --> 01:16:05.020
because that was the system's
means of social reproduction.

01:16:05.290 --> 01:16:07.947
- [Catherine] Right, that's
what I was thinking, exactly.

01:16:07.947 --> 01:16:10.283
- That's how it is that you,

01:16:11.840 --> 01:16:15.200
and the only way we have to
really take the measure of it

01:16:15.200 --> 01:16:19.010
is demographic scholarship and research,

01:16:19.010 --> 01:16:21.100
that, I mean, I try to teach this stuff

01:16:21.100 --> 01:16:22.693
and my students' eyes just glaze over it,

01:16:22.693 --> 01:16:24.300
because it's dry stuff.

01:16:24.300 --> 01:16:28.610
But it's the demographic
data is the reflection

01:16:28.610 --> 01:16:31.480
of what does it mean to have populations

01:16:31.480 --> 01:16:34.083
that cannot reproduce
themselves biologically?

01:16:35.230 --> 01:16:37.960
And so every time a slave ship comes back,

01:16:37.960 --> 01:16:40.150
you know, and that's why
it's so compelling to me

01:16:40.150 --> 01:16:43.402
to get this planter Cary Helyar to say,

01:16:43.402 --> 01:16:46.600
"I'm gonna make these
slaves buy more slaves."

01:16:46.600 --> 01:16:48.770
I mean, that is the logic.

01:16:48.770 --> 01:16:49.873
That's the system.

01:16:52.390 --> 01:16:54.480
- I'm going to skip a little bit down

01:16:54.480 --> 01:16:57.060
to some questions that are more on topic.

01:16:57.060 --> 01:16:59.810
I'm not ignoring the people
with broader questions,

01:16:59.810 --> 01:17:01.770
but I wanna focus around the talk

01:17:01.770 --> 01:17:03.860
at least first, if that's okay.

01:17:03.860 --> 01:17:06.073
I'm gonna read the
question for Berto Marotta

01:17:06.073 --> 01:17:09.240
whose microphone is out.

01:17:09.240 --> 01:17:11.817
Question is, "How would
you articulate the relation

01:17:11.817 --> 01:17:14.527
"between the appropriation of slave labor

01:17:14.527 --> 01:17:17.175
"and the appropriation of nature,

01:17:17.175 --> 01:17:18.750
"the ecological nature?"
- The appropriation of what?

01:17:18.750 --> 01:17:20.400
- Nature.

01:17:20.400 --> 01:17:23.510
Which Berto is calling ecological surplus

01:17:23.510 --> 01:17:25.023
in the longue duree.

01:17:27.383 --> 01:17:30.500
Alienation of labor, alienation of nature.

01:17:30.500 --> 01:17:35.500
- How would I characterize the
relationship between the two?

01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:37.670
- Yeah, I'm not sure what
the question is getting at,

01:17:37.670 --> 01:17:39.920
but let's speculate.

01:17:39.920 --> 01:17:41.070
- Hm.

01:17:41.070 --> 01:17:46.070
Well, it's not a question I
have given a lot of thought to.

01:17:49.480 --> 01:17:52.050
I guess, thinking off the top of my head,

01:17:52.050 --> 01:17:56.793
I would say that were I to pursue it,

01:17:58.540 --> 01:18:00.720
I think it would lead me again

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:05.720
to taking more seriously

01:18:06.180 --> 01:18:10.860
than I think we have in
historical scholarship

01:18:10.860 --> 01:18:13.943
what are the logics of colonizing?

01:18:15.160 --> 01:18:19.070
You know, what are the costs?

01:18:19.070 --> 01:18:21.963
What are the technologies
of dispossession?

01:18:24.770 --> 01:18:29.770
What are the environmental
consequences of plantation labor?

01:18:31.430 --> 01:18:35.130
And there are scholarship that
is beginning to pursue this.

01:18:35.130 --> 01:18:37.383
I'm not caught up on it to be blunt,

01:18:39.440 --> 01:18:42.490
but I think there's a literature emerging

01:18:42.490 --> 01:18:46.513
that correlates to the question.

01:18:49.550 --> 01:18:51.850
- Why don't we go on?
- It's a good question.

01:18:51.850 --> 01:18:54.043
It's a good avenue to pursue.

01:18:56.430 --> 01:19:00.810
- Next question on the
list is from Deonte Harris.

01:19:00.810 --> 01:19:02.397
Can we unmute Deonte?

01:19:03.310 --> 01:19:07.560
This is a question kind of
solidly in your wheelhouse.

01:19:07.560 --> 01:19:08.750
- [Deonte] Yes, I am here.

01:19:08.750 --> 01:19:10.790
Thank you so much Dr.
Smallwood for this talk.

01:19:10.790 --> 01:19:13.018
I hope that you're able to hear me okay.

01:19:13.018 --> 01:19:13.851
- [Dr. Smallwood] Yes.

01:19:13.851 --> 01:19:16.510
- [Deonte] The question I had was thinking

01:19:16.510 --> 01:19:19.750
about processes of
commodification and also thinking

01:19:19.750 --> 01:19:23.270
about labor power and wanting
to have more discussion

01:19:23.270 --> 01:19:26.900
about the differences between
commodification of things

01:19:26.900 --> 01:19:29.900
versus the commodification
of human beings, right.

01:19:29.900 --> 01:19:34.300
And also how then to analyze
these relationally together

01:19:34.300 --> 01:19:37.610
to understand that there were
multiple processes happening

01:19:37.610 --> 01:19:40.120
at the same time, which
makes this particular system

01:19:40.120 --> 01:19:44.180
so interesting, unique and
horrifying at the same time.

01:19:44.180 --> 01:19:46.633
So if you could speak a bit to,

01:19:47.490 --> 01:19:50.150
about this process of
commodifying human beings

01:19:50.150 --> 01:19:51.270
in this Atlantic system

01:19:51.270 --> 01:19:53.090
and how then their combined labor powers

01:19:53.090 --> 01:19:55.609
then to I guess use

01:19:55.609 --> 01:19:59.960
to generate or create more commodities

01:19:59.960 --> 01:20:02.253
for the international market.

01:20:05.920 --> 01:20:07.390
- Yeah.

01:20:07.390 --> 01:20:09.520
The commodification of people,

01:20:09.520 --> 01:20:14.520
that's where my research took off.

01:20:14.640 --> 01:20:18.773
And the book that I
wrote on the slave trade.

01:20:20.480 --> 01:20:25.270
It was when I went into the
archives of the slave trade,

01:20:25.270 --> 01:20:26.773
I would ask myself,

01:20:28.027 --> 01:20:31.157
"What have I learned that
I didn't already know

01:20:31.157 --> 01:20:34.140
"about the slave trade and how it worked?"

01:20:34.140 --> 01:20:38.090
And that literally was my method.

01:20:38.090 --> 01:20:42.380
And it's what led to the
structure of the book,

01:20:42.380 --> 01:20:47.380
which became a story organized
around commodification

01:20:48.690 --> 01:20:53.460
as how people became slaves

01:20:53.460 --> 01:20:58.280
and how the system achieved the efficiency

01:21:00.290 --> 01:21:01.510
that it did achieve.

01:21:01.510 --> 01:21:05.610
Within the context of the
17th and 18th centuries,

01:21:05.610 --> 01:21:08.040
it was an efficient system, I would say.

01:21:08.040 --> 01:21:10.810
Again, that's how it did

01:21:10.810 --> 01:21:14.040
what it shouldn't have been able to do,

01:21:14.040 --> 01:21:19.040
which was to displace so
many millions of people.

01:21:20.140 --> 01:21:24.820
And so, you know, I think in
that sense, the commodification

01:21:24.820 --> 01:21:29.820
of people really reflected a logic

01:21:29.910 --> 01:21:32.290
that became very comfortable.

01:21:32.290 --> 01:21:33.923
And this is how it becomes,

01:21:36.070 --> 01:21:38.580
you know, and I'm thinking of
Cedric Robinson's work here.

01:21:38.580 --> 01:21:42.650
This is how it becomes a
differentiating logic, right?

01:21:42.650 --> 01:21:46.170
A logic that is without necessarily even

01:21:46.170 --> 01:21:48.750
yet producing what we would recognize

01:21:48.750 --> 01:21:51.040
as obvious discourses of race,

01:21:51.040 --> 01:21:54.000
but producing certainly a logic

01:21:54.000 --> 01:21:59.000
of some humans being more readily

01:21:59.280 --> 01:22:02.880
and more appropriately treated

01:22:02.880 --> 01:22:07.880
as though they are things than others.

01:22:07.890 --> 01:22:11.790
And so it's not particularly different

01:22:11.790 --> 01:22:14.850
than the commodification of things,

01:22:14.850 --> 01:22:19.850
except the oddity, the peculiarity,

01:22:22.630 --> 01:22:25.690
to return to the Marxian formulation,

01:22:25.690 --> 01:22:27.460
of the human as a commodity

01:22:27.460 --> 01:22:29.743
is that it's a perishable commodity.

01:22:31.400 --> 01:22:35.230
And there are some things
that share that quality,

01:22:35.230 --> 01:22:40.230
but that is also part of what
becomes so profoundly inhumane

01:22:42.870 --> 01:22:46.430
in the violence of colonial slavery

01:22:46.430 --> 01:22:50.850
is the kinds of choices

01:22:50.850 --> 01:22:54.920
and the kinds of violence and destruction

01:22:57.840 --> 01:23:02.420
that has to be naturalized and normalized

01:23:02.420 --> 01:23:07.420
in order to produce people
as though they are things.

01:23:09.400 --> 01:23:14.250
So to reduce, for instance, people

01:23:14.250 --> 01:23:19.250
to one of four demographic categories

01:23:19.540 --> 01:23:23.120
of either being a man or a
woman or a boy or a girl,

01:23:23.120 --> 01:23:28.120
that kind of evacuation of
all qualities of personhood

01:23:29.550 --> 01:23:34.550
in order to reduce a crowd
of people to a slave cargo,

01:23:38.940 --> 01:23:43.940
so that a slave ship can
continue its scheduled movement

01:23:44.250 --> 01:23:46.063
in transatlantic circuits.

01:23:49.080 --> 01:23:51.810
I hope that begins to answer the question.

01:23:51.810 --> 01:23:54.110
- [Deonte] Absolutely,
thank you so very much.

01:23:55.790 --> 01:23:56.623
- Thanks.

01:23:56.623 --> 01:23:58.830
I want to move to another question

01:23:58.830 --> 01:24:01.050
by my colleague, Lisa Lowe,

01:24:01.050 --> 01:24:03.730
Who's been invoked a
couple of times tonight.

01:24:03.730 --> 01:24:05.150
We will give her the microphone.

01:24:05.150 --> 01:24:07.860
Audrey, would you unmute Professor Lowe?

01:24:07.860 --> 01:24:08.693
Thank you.

01:24:09.930 --> 01:24:12.283
- Thank you for your beautiful talk.

01:24:13.690 --> 01:24:17.700
I sense that your critique of
Marx has much greater stakes

01:24:17.700 --> 01:24:22.590
than simply correcting
his analysis of capitalism

01:24:22.590 --> 01:24:23.980
in an empirical sense,

01:24:23.980 --> 01:24:26.660
though, I'm persuaded that his reduction

01:24:26.660 --> 01:24:30.970
of slavery as primitive
accumulation does obscure the scale

01:24:30.970 --> 01:24:33.610
and periodization and
the nature of violence

01:24:33.610 --> 01:24:36.690
and the bioavailability
of the enslaved body

01:24:36.690 --> 01:24:40.330
and the forms of extraction and so forth.

01:24:40.330 --> 01:24:43.010
But I wanted to ask you what you think,

01:24:43.010 --> 01:24:44.690
if you think there's a material

01:24:44.690 --> 01:24:49.690
or political effect or afterlife
of this mistaken reduction,

01:24:50.747 --> 01:24:55.747
the missing of slavery as labor
or surplus value circulating

01:24:56.620 --> 01:24:59.783
within a global labor market,

01:25:00.850 --> 01:25:04.410
and what the consequences
of periodizing slavery

01:25:04.410 --> 01:25:06.100
as primitive accumulation

01:25:06.100 --> 01:25:08.260
or prior to capitalism might be?

01:25:08.260 --> 01:25:13.260
What does that say about what we can't see

01:25:13.750 --> 01:25:16.720
about the continuation
of primitive accumulation

01:25:17.710 --> 01:25:21.430
as co-evil or continuous with?

01:25:21.430 --> 01:25:22.870
- Yeah, yeah.

01:25:22.870 --> 01:25:24.900
Thank you for that question, Lisa.

01:25:24.900 --> 01:25:28.300
Well, I think for me, the stakes are that,

01:25:28.300 --> 01:25:31.050
and I guess I was trying to
gesture at this a little bit

01:25:31.050 --> 01:25:35.113
in the very, the last few
sentences of the talk.

01:25:36.080 --> 01:25:40.600
You know, I think if we were to consider

01:25:40.600 --> 01:25:45.600
that colonial slavery is squarely

01:25:45.750 --> 01:25:49.840
within the ambit of the history

01:25:49.840 --> 01:25:52.693
of capital C capitalism,

01:25:53.590 --> 01:25:56.350
it would mean not only a rethinking

01:25:56.350 --> 01:26:01.260
of the relationship of slavery
to industrial capitalization,

01:26:01.260 --> 01:26:03.300
but it would entail a rethinking

01:26:03.300 --> 01:26:07.830
of slavery's relationship to our present.

01:26:07.830 --> 01:26:09.820
I mean, I think it would allow us

01:26:09.820 --> 01:26:12.587
to understand capitalism today

01:26:16.190 --> 01:26:21.190
as the continued playing
out of colonial slavery.

01:26:21.870 --> 01:26:24.280
It would allow us to see ourselves

01:26:24.280 --> 01:26:29.230
as part of a genealogy
that begins with slavery.

01:26:29.230 --> 01:26:33.500
It would make slavery
part of our genealogy.

01:26:33.500 --> 01:26:38.500
And I think that that has
profound, productive implications.

01:26:40.400 --> 01:26:41.303
- [Lisa] Thank you.

01:26:46.320 --> 01:26:47.153
- Okay.

01:26:47.153 --> 01:26:49.380
Yes, I was getting

01:26:49.380 --> 01:26:52.410
on a subchannel a text
from a graduate student

01:26:52.410 --> 01:26:55.000
which is something I wanted to add too,

01:26:55.000 --> 01:27:00.000
which is that it really
makes the whole metaphoric

01:27:00.070 --> 01:27:01.600
in "Capital" stand out.

01:27:01.600 --> 01:27:03.720
This is from a graduate student.

01:27:03.720 --> 01:27:05.010
She sent this privately,

01:27:05.010 --> 01:27:06.960
but I'm gonna out her, Vanessa Gubbins.

01:27:07.820 --> 01:27:11.900
Every time Marx says it's like slaves,

01:27:11.900 --> 01:27:13.150
wage labor is slavery.

01:27:13.150 --> 01:27:13.983
It's all there.

01:27:13.983 --> 01:27:16.370
It's right on the surface of the text.

01:27:16.370 --> 01:27:18.550
- Yeah, it's haunted by slavery.

01:27:18.550 --> 01:27:19.990
- It's really haunted by slavery.

01:27:19.990 --> 01:27:23.183
Hard to imagine he's thinking
of Rome in those instances.

01:27:25.760 --> 01:27:30.760
Well, thank you very much
for this kind presence,

01:27:31.140 --> 01:27:33.642
a brilliant opening up of the text,

01:27:33.642 --> 01:27:38.642
a stand on a day that's really
very difficult for everyone.

01:27:40.080 --> 01:27:41.490
We really appreciate your coming,

01:27:41.490 --> 01:27:45.810
and we hope that people take
up this challenge you've given

01:27:45.810 --> 01:27:48.440
to fill in the gaps and
make the connections

01:27:48.440 --> 01:27:52.050
and give us a better
picture of our genealogy.

01:27:52.050 --> 01:27:53.570
So thank you so much.

01:27:53.570 --> 01:27:55.560
- Thank you very much for having me.

01:27:55.560 --> 01:27:56.393
- Thank you.

01:27:56.393 --> 01:27:58.415
This is another moment of big applause.

01:27:58.415 --> 01:28:00.540
(claps) This would sound very loud.

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:01.580
Thank you.

01:28:01.580 --> 01:28:02.413
- Thanks.

